<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Neuchâtel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> is the largest lake lying entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, in the west of the country at the foot of the Jura, with the cities of Neuchâtel and Yverdon on its shores. The site of La Tène at its northern end gave its name to the Celtic culture of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and the lake is rich in prehistoric lake-dwelling sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-neuchatel.jpg" alt="Lake Neuchâtel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lac_de_neuchatel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> lies mainly in Romandy, the French-speaking part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, at the foot of the Jura mountains on the Swiss Plateau. It is shared by the cantons of Neuchâtel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>, Fribourg and Bern and forms part of the Three Lakes Region with Lake Biel/Bienne and Lake Morat/Murten. It has a surface of 218.3 square kilometres, is 38.3 km long and 8.2 km at its widest, lies 429 metres above sea level and is at most 152 metres deep. It holds about 14.0 cubic kilometres of water and is the largest lake entirely within Switzerland, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> are shared with neighbouring countries.</p><p>The lake is fed by the Orbe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnon">Arnon</a>, the Areuse, the Seyon and the Menthue and by the Broye canal, and the Thielle canal drains it into Lake Biel. Since the Jura water correction of the 19th and 20th centuries, it has served with Lake Morat as a compensation basin for the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> flowing into Lake Biel. The extinct deepwater trout Salvelinus neocomensis once lived in it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric people frequented the lake, as remains at Auvernier and in the Laténium archaeological museum show; bones of brown bear and Eurasian beaver were found there. Megalithic monuments line the shore, including the alignment of Clendy and the menhirs of Gorgier, Grandson, Saint-Aubin-Sauges and Vauroux. A Celtic settlement on pile-dwellings at La Tène gave its name to the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. Mont Vully, a fortified area of about 50 hectares built around 120 BCE, controlled the lakes of Morat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> while the La Tène area stayed almost unoccupied. Yverdon, then Eburodunum, was settled from the 4th century BCE, fortified in 80 BCE, and became a vicus in the first centuries CE.</p><p>The first written mention of the lake dates from 998 CE, as the laci everdunensis, the lake of Yverdon, near which the priory of Bevaix was founded. That name dominated through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the name Lake Neuchâtel appears from the 15th century and became dominant in the 19th, with the lowering of the lake's level and the Vaudois railway, which reduced the importance of the Port of Yverdon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Settlements on the north-western shore run from Yverdon-les-Bains through Grandson, Concise and Bevaix to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> and Marin-Epagnier, and those on the south-eastern shore include Estavayer-le-Lac, Cudrefin and Gampelen. Neuchâtel became a popular tourist destination in the Belle Époque for its climate and its panoramic views of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and the lake's shore has seen significant economic development since the regional motorway network was completed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Neuchâtel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 450 BCE the La Tène culture took its place, named after a site on <b>Lake Neuchâtel</b> in Switzerland where many weapons and other objects had been placed in the water.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The La Tène culture is named after its type site on the north shore of <b>Lake Neuchâtel</b> in Switzerland, where thousands of objects had been deposited in the lake.</blockquote><a href="/culture/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Swiss Confederates routed the Burgundian army near <b>Lake Neuchâtel</b> and seized Charles the Bold's rich baggage train.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The site of La Tène on <b>Lake Neuchâtel</b>, with its weapons and offerings, has given its name to the late Iron Age culture of Celtic Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li></ul></section>
